Real code:

private FoodItem spawnFood() {
    Point position;
    do {
        position = new Point(random.nextInt(board.getHeight()), random.nextInt(board.getWidth()));
    } while (occupied.contains(position));
    return foodSpawnStrategy.nextFoodCreator().spawnAt(position);
}

This loop keeps drawing random cells until it finds one the snake is not on. On a 2x1 board, a snake occupying one cell eats the food in the other. Running exactly that: the program printed 'food at (0,1), now eating it...' and then never returned. What happened?,The snake grew too large and overflowed the Deque,"Every cell is now occupied, so the do/while can never find a free position and spins forever - the game hangs instead of ending",spawnFood() threw an exception that was swallowed,The random number generator ran out of values,,2,"Verified by running it with a four-second timeout: still spinning. Filling the board is normally a WIN, but this design has no concept of one, so the success case is an infinite loop.",2

Real code:

private FoodItem spawnFood() {
    Point position;
    do {
        position = new Point(random.nextInt(board.getHeight()), random.nextInt(board.getWidth()));
    } while (occupied.contains(position));
    return foodSpawnStrategy.nextFoodCreator().spawnAt(position);
}

Following on from the hang: what would fix it properly?,"Add a counter and give up after 1000 attempts, leaving the food where it is","Check whether any free cell exists before drawing - if the snake fills the board, end the game as a win instead of searching for a position that cannot exist",Make the loop a while loop instead of a do/while,Catch the exception thrown when the board is full,,2,"A retry cap turns a hang into a different bug. The real gap is that 'board full' is a legitimate end state the design never modelled, so there is nothing sensible for the loop to fall back on.",2

Real code:

private FoodItem spawnFood() {
    Point position;
    do {
        position = new Point(random.nextInt(board.getHeight()), random.nextInt(board.getWidth()));
    } while (occupied.contains(position));
    return foodSpawnStrategy.nextFoodCreator().spawnAt(position);
}

Setting the full-board case aside, this loop also gets slower as the snake grows. Why, and when does it start to matter?,It does not - each attempt is constant time,"Each draw is constant time, but as the snake covers more of the board a larger share of draws land on the snake and are retried - on a nearly-full board it may take many attempts to find the few free cells",Because occupied.contains() gets slower as the Set grows,Because the Random object degrades after many calls,,2,"Fine on a 6x6 board with a short snake, which is why the demos never notice. The cost is in the number of retries, not in any single attempt.",2

Real code:

public class CyclingFoodSpawnStrategy implements IFoodSpawnStrategy {

    private final FoodCreator[] cycle = { new NormalFoodCreator(), new BonusFoodCreator(), new SuperFoodCreator() };
    private int spawnCount = 0;

    @Override
    public FoodCreator nextFoodCreator() {
        return cycle[spawnCount++ % cycle.length];
    }
}

One instance of this strategy is created and passed to two games in a row. Calling it four times in sequence returned Normal, Bonus, Super, Normal. What does the second game experience?,"It starts the cycle from Normal, like the first game","It continues wherever the first game stopped - if the first game ate two foods, the second game's first food is a Super rather than a Normal","It throws, because the array index runs past the end","Both games share food, so they always see the same type",,2,"The requirement says no state may leak between games. spawnCount is exactly that leak, and nothing in the API warns the caller.",2

Real code:

public void performMove(Direction direction) {
    Point currentHead = body.peekFirst();
    Point rawNewHead = new Point(currentHead.getRow() + direction.getDeltaRow(),
            currentHead.getCol() + direction.getDeltaCol());

    Point newHead = handleBoundary(rawNewHead);   // <-- the one varying step
    if (newHead == null) {
        endGame();
        return;
    }

    Point tail = body.peekLast();
    boolean eatsFood = newHead.equals(currentFood.getPosition());
    boolean bitesItself = occupied.contains(newHead) && !(newHead.equals(tail));

    if (bitesItself) {
        endGame();
        return;
    }

    if (eatsFood) {
        body.addFirst(newHead);
        occupied.add(newHead);
        score += scoringRule.pointsFor(currentFood);
        currentFood = spawnFood();
        notifyScoreChanged();
    } else {
        body.pollLast();
        occupied.remove(tail);
        body.addFirst(newHead);
        occupied.add(newHead);
    }
}

protected abstract Point handleBoundary(Point rawNewHead);

// SnakeGame
private IScoringRule scoringRule = new BaseScoringRule();

public void setScoringRule(IScoringRule scoringRule) {
    this.scoringRule = scoringRule;
}

setScoringRule() is public and can be called at any moment, including mid-game. What can a caller do with it that they almost certainly should not?,Nothing - replacing the rule is always safe,"Replace the whole chain rather than wrapping it, silently discarding any active power-ups

Real code:

public interface IScoringRule {
    int pointsFor(FoodItem food);
    void onMove();
}

public abstract class ScoringRuleDecorator implements IScoringRule {
    protected final IScoringRule wrapped;

    @Override
    public int pointsFor(FoodItem food) { return wrapped.pointsFor(food); }

    @Override
    public void onMove() { wrapped.onMove(); }
}

public class DoublePointsDecorator extends ScoringRuleDecorator {
    private int movesRemaining;

    @Override
    public int pointsFor(FoodItem food) {
        int base = super.pointsFor(food);
        return isActive() ? base * 2 : base;
    }

    @Override
    public void onMove() {
        super.onMove();
        if (movesRemaining > 0) movesRemaining--;
    }
}

A DoublePointsDecorator whose movesRemaining has reached zero still sits in the chain and is still called on every move and every scored food. What is the consequence over a long game with many power-ups?,The score is doubled permanently,"Nothing changes behaviourally, but the chain keeps getting longer - each expired decorator still delegates on every call, and nothing ever removes them",Each expired decorator throws once it reaches zero,The oldest decorator is dropped automatically,,2,"Harmless at this scale and worth naming honestly: layering with no unwrapping path means the structure only grows. A rule that reports itself expired, plus a prune step, would be the fix.",2

