onMove() is reached only on the last line of performMove(). Both endGame() paths return before it. Does a move that ends the game count against a power-up's remaining moves?,Yes - endGame() ticks the rule before returning,"No, and it does not matter: once the game is over no further scoring happens, so whether that final move was counted can never be observed","No, and this is a bug that shortchanges the player","Yes, because the tick happens in the state class instead",,2,"Worth practising the distinction between an inconsistency and a defect. This one is unobservable, so it costs nothing - unlike the occupied/body drift, which was equally invisible at the moment it happened but corrupted later moves.",2

Real code:

public class DoublePointsDecorator extends ScoringRuleDecorator {

    public DoublePointsDecorator(IScoringRule wrapped, int durationInMoves) {
        super(wrapped);
        this.movesRemaining = durationInMoves;
    }
}

// how it is applied
game.setScoringRule(new DoublePointsDecorator(game.getScoringRule(), 12));

In the recorded run this produced scores of 2, 8 and 18 for foods worth 1, 3 and 5, then 19, 22 and 27 for the same three foods afterwards. What do those two groups show?,"The power-up doubled the first three foods and then expired, after which the same foods scored their face value",The power-up doubled every food for the whole game,The power-up added a flat 1 point per food,The scores were random because the food order changed,,2,"First three gains are 2, 6 and 10 - exactly double 1, 3 and 5. The next three are 1, 3 and 5. A second game with no power-up produced 1, 4, 9, 10, 13, 18, which is the same food sequence undoubled.",2

Real code:

public class ClassicSnakeGame extends SnakeGame {

    @Override
    protected Point handleBoundary(Point rawNewHead) {
        boolean withinBounds = getBoard().isWithinBounds(rawNewHead.getRow(), rawNewHead.getCol());
        return withinBounds ? rawNewHead : null;   // null -> hitting the wall ends the game
    }
}

// requirements.txt section 9
// A new food kind, a new boundary mode, and a new score listener must each be
// addable in isolation, not as edits scattered across working code.

Three of the four stretch requirements were met purely by adding files. The power-up was not. What did it need that the others did not?,A new subclass of SnakeGame,"A seam that did not exist yet - scoring was hardcoded as 'score += currentFood.getPoints()' inside performMove(), so SnakeGame had to be edited to introduce IScoringRule before anything could be layered on",A change to the Board class,Nothing - it was also added purely by adding files,,2,"An honest result worth teaching: extension points only exist where somebody put one. Food, boundary and listeners each had a seam already

