getHead(), getFoodPosition(), getScore() and getLength() are all public, and the greedy bot in Main calls getHead() and getFoodPosition() before every move to decide where to go. What does that make possible that a real player could not do?,Nothing - a player can see the board too,"The bot reads exact positions instantly and never mis-steers, so it dies only from a genuine trap rather than from reaction time - which is what makes the demo runs reproducible",The bot can move two cells per turn,The bot can see through the snake's body,,2,"The reason for pointing this out is that the recorded run is only reproducible because nothing about it is timing-dependent - the seed fixes the food, and the bot's decisions are a pure function of the state.",1

Real code:

// Main.java
private static Direction pickGreedyDirection(Point head, Point food) {
    if (food.getRow() < head.getRow()) return Direction.UP;
    if (food.getRow() > head.getRow()) return Direction.DOWN;
    if (food.getCol() < head.getCol()) return Direction.LEFT;
    return Direction.RIGHT;
}

This bot always closes the row gap before the column gap and never considers its own body. In the recorded run it died at length 8 by stepping into a segment it had laid down two moves earlier. Is the bot buggy?,Yes - it should be fixed so the demo runs forever,"No. It is deliberately naive: it exists to drive the demo deterministically, not to play well, and its death is a genuine self-collision that exercises the game-over path",Yes - it should at minimum avoid reversing into itself,"No, because a greedy strategy is provably optimal for snake",,2,Worth separating test scaffolding from the system under test. A smarter bot would make the demo longer without testing anything the current one misses.,1

Real code:

public class Board {
    public boolean isWithinBounds(int row, int col) {
        return row >= 0 && row < height && col >= 0 && col < width;
    }
}

// Board
public Board(int width, int height) {
    this.width = width;
    this.height = height;
}

// Main
Board board = new Board(6, 6);
SnakeGame game = new ClassicSnakeGame(board, new Point(3, 3), spawnStrategy, random);

The Board constructor takes (width, height) while Point takes (row, col) - and rows map to height while columns map to width. What risk does that create, and why has it not bitten yet?,No risk - the compiler catches mismatches,"The two orders are reversed relative to each other, so a non-square board could easily be constructed or indexed the wrong way round. Every demo board is square (6x6, 5x5, 8x8), which hides the mistake completely","No risk, because Board validates its arguments",The risk is that Point should store width and height instead,,2,"Two adjacent int parameters with no type distinction is a classic trap, and square test data is very good at concealing it. A 6x3 board would settle it immediately - as would a Dimension type, or naming the constructor parameters in a builder.",2

